# MLServe: Quick Start

```elixir
Mix.install([
  # Running from a clone of the repo. Against the published package this is:
  #   {:ml_serve, "~> 0.1.0"}
  {:ml_serve, path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)},
  {:kino, "~> 0.14"}
])
```

## What this is

MLServe is the runtime layer around a model, not a modelling library. It does not train, it does
not own a tensor type, and it does not wrap an LLM HTTP API. It supplies the parts every team
ends up rebuilding by hand when a model goes into a Phoenix application: supervision, worker
pools, batching, caching, telemetry, versioning and safe rollout.

This notebook takes about five minutes. By the end you will have loaded a model, served
predictions from it, read its live operational status and unloaded it — with nothing installed
beyond `:telemetry`.

## A model is two functions

The entire `MLServe.Model` behaviour is `load/1` and `predict/2`. Everything else —
`batch_predict/2`, `unload/1`, `metadata/1`, `capabilities/0` — is optional and detected at load
time with `function_exported?/3`.

There is no ML runtime below. That is the point: MLServe cannot tell the difference between a
neural network and an `if` statement, and applies the same pooling, batching, caching and
versioning to both.

```elixir
defmodule FraudModel do
  @behaviour MLServe.Model

  @impl true
  def load(config) do
    # Whatever load/1 returns becomes the state passed to every predict/2 call. A real backend
    # opens its ONNX session or builds its Nx.Serving here, so the cost is paid once at load
    # rather than once per request.
    {:ok, %{threshold: Keyword.fetch!(config, :threshold)}}
  end

  @impl true
  def predict(%{threshold: threshold}, features) do
    %{amount: amount} = features
    failed = Map.get(features, :failed_transactions_24h, 0)

    probability =
      (amount / 2_000 + failed * 0.05)
      |> min(1.0)
      |> Float.round(4)

    {:ok,
     %{
       prediction: if(probability > threshold, do: :fraud, else: :legitimate),
       probability: probability
     }}
  end

  # Optional. Surfaces under :metadata in MLServe.model_status/2 — a good place to record what
  # the model was trained on.
  @impl true
  def metadata(%{threshold: threshold}) do
    %{threshold: threshold, features: [:amount, :failed_transactions_24h]}
  end
end
```

## Loading it

Models can be declared in `config :ml_serve, :models` or loaded at runtime. Both go through the
same validation and the same code path — configuration is not a privileged route.

`load_model/2` returns as soon as the model is **registered**. Loading itself proceeds in the
background under `handle_continue/2`, so a thirty-second model load never blocks application
boot. That is why `await_ready/2` exists, and why a real service exposes a readiness endpoint
rather than assuming.

```elixir
{:ok, {name, version}} =
  MLServe.load_model(:fraud_detection,
    backend: FraudModel,
    version: "1.0.0",
    workers: 4,
    config: [threshold: 0.7]
  )

:ok = MLServe.await_ready(:fraud_detection)

{name, version, MLServe.ready?(:fraud_detection)}
```

## Predicting

`predict/3` returns `{:ok, result}` or `{:error, reason}` and never raises for an expected
failure, so it composes with `with`. `predict!/3` raises `MLServe.Error` instead, for the places
where you would only re-raise anyway.

```elixir
MLServe.predict(:fraud_detection, %{
  amount: 1500.50,
  transaction_count_24h: 8,
  failed_transactions_24h: 2
})
```

Try a few amounts at once and look at where the threshold bites:

```elixir
[10.0, 500.0, 1_200.0, 1_500.0, 2_500.0]
|> Enum.map(fn amount ->
  {:ok, result} = MLServe.predict(:fraud_detection, %{amount: amount})
  %{amount: amount, prediction: result.prediction, probability: result.probability}
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

## Many inputs at once

`batch_predict/3` hands the whole list to the backend in one call when it implements
`batch_predict/2`, and otherwise maps `predict/2` over it. Your call site does not change either
way — which means adding `batch_predict/2` to a backend later is a pure optimisation, invisible
to callers.

```elixir
{:ok, results} =
  MLServe.batch_predict(:fraud_detection, [
    %{amount: 10.0},
    %{amount: 900.0},
    %{amount: 5_000.0}
  ])

results
```

## Operational status

`requests`, `errors` and `in_flight` come from atomic counters written on the hot path. Reading
them asks no process anything, so a `/health` endpoint can call this on every request without a
second thought.

```elixir
{:ok, status} = MLServe.model_status(:fraud_detection)

Kino.DataTable.new([
  %{field: "status", value: inspect(status.status)},
  %{field: "backend", value: inspect(status.backend)},
  %{field: "concurrency", value: inspect(status.concurrency)},
  %{field: "workers", value: inspect(status.workers)},
  %{field: "requests", value: inspect(status.requests)},
  %{field: "errors", value: inspect(status.errors)},
  %{field: "in flight", value: inspect(status.in_flight)},
  %{field: "loaded in", value: "#{status.load_duration_ms}ms"},
  %{field: "default?", value: inspect(status.default?)},
  %{field: "metadata", value: inspect(status.metadata)}
])
```

## Unloading

`unload_model/2` waits up to the model's `:drain_timeout` for in-flight requests to finish before
tearing its supervision subtree down.

```elixir
:ok = MLServe.unload_model(:fraud_detection)

MLServe.predict(:fraud_detection, %{amount: 1.0})
```

